export const PASSAGES = {"item:a-bloodstained-journal-page":[["item:a-bloodstained-journal-page#section2","Handwriting and Guilt","To remove any shred of doubt before enacting justice, Commander Ebonlocke relies on Clerk Daltry, keeper of Darkshire's Town Hall registry. Years spent archiving municipal signatures made Daltry an authority on local penmanship. Upon examining the bloodstained leaf against municipal records, Daltry definitively authenticates the hand as Stalvan's, sealing the tutor's guilt.",["q66.6"]]],"item:a-mysterious-message":[["item:a-mysterious-message#section2","Intercepted Orders","A courier journeying between Defias operations at Moonbrook, the Gold Coast Quarry, and the Jangolode Mine carries the message. Its interception marks a break in Gryan Stoutmantle's investigation. For weeks, the People's Militia suspected someone was directing the bandit uprising, but had no sure proof. The missive confirms that Edwin VanCleef commands the brotherhood's efforts across Westfall through coded ranks and intermediaries.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:arikara-serpent-skin":[["item:arikara-serpent-skin#section2","A Troubling Revelation","When brought to Motega Firemane at Whitereach Post, the skin proves the serpent's death but raises uncomfortable questions. Magatha Grimtotem insisted Arikara specifically hunted Cairne Bloodhoof out of ancestral rage, yet examining the slain creature leaves Motega bewildered as to why such a spirit would truly threaten their High Chieftain. Though the pelt settles the immediate danger, it leaves a lingering suspicion over Magatha's true interpretations.",["q4821.5"]]],"item:ataeric-s-staff":[["item:ataeric-s-staff#section2","A Conduit of Ley Magic","Carried by Archmage Ataeric, this staff was used to direct and harness ley energies in southern Silverpine Forest. Under Dalaran's direction, Ataeric attempted to reactivate an ancient ley energy node near the borders of the magocracy's territory. The staff acted as a vital tool for weaving these immense ambient currents, rendering the archmage a formidable threat to nearby Forsaken settlements.",["q477.3"]]],"item:athrikus-narassin-s-head":[["item:athrikus-narassin-s-head#section1","Blight Upon Althalaxx","Perched at the pinnacle of the Tower of Althalaxx, Athrikus Narassin commanded a coven of dark sorcerers practicing fel magic along the Darkshore coast. Ancient and entrenched, Narassin coordinated demonic summonings that threatened the surrounding wilderness. Claiming his head shatters the coven's leadership, driving back their immediate foothold in the region, even if the lingering corruption promises future vigilance will be needed.",["q954.5"]]],"item:bethor-s-scroll":[["item:bethor-s-scroll#section2","A Colleague's Betrayal","Upon examining tainted ichor recovered from Rot Hide gnolls, Bethor Iceshard recognized the handiwork of Thule Ravenclaw. Once a promising young mage of the Kirin Tor, Thule betrayed his brethren to serve the Lich King when the Scourge rose to power. Learning that Thule had survived and was directing Rot Hide attacks across Silverpine Forest from Fenris Isle, Bethor drew upon his intimate knowledge of his former colleague's spellcraft to author this scroll.",["q437.6"]]],"item:crude-flint":[["item:crude-flint#section1","Kindling in the Mire","While crude and unworked, these simple striking flints provide the Mosshide gnolls with their primary means of generating heat and cooking meat amidst the damp bogs of the Wetlands. Unfortunately for the marsh's ecosystem, the Mosshides fell living timber to feed their hearths and bonfires. This reckless harvesting and combustion of living boughs caught the ire of Rethiel the Greenwarden, who perceives each spark struck as an assault against the natural balance.",["q463.2"]],["item:crude-flint#section2","The Greenwarden's Retribution","Determined to stop the slaughter of the marsh's trees, Rethiel tasks passing adventurers with systematically stripping the Mosshide pack of their firestones. Rather than repurposing the scavenged flint, the guardian plans to smash every retrieved piece to dust and cast the fragments into the sea, ensuring the gnolls can no longer burn the Wetlands' living wood.",["q463.2"]]],"item:dusky-crab-cakes":[["item:dusky-crab-cakes#section1","A Questionable Delicacy","In the gloom of Duskwood, fresh provisions are scarce and cooks must make do with whatever meat the darkened forest yields. When the terrified vagrant Jitters finds himself racked with hunger pains, Chef Grual whips up a batch of his signature 'crab' cakes. To any outsider, the dish appears to be an innocent seafood patty, but Grual's secret ingredient is far less appetizing: the sinewy, gooey legs of Duskwood's monstrous arachnids. The cakes are quickly devoured by the ravenous Jitters, completely oblivious to what he is actually eating.",["q5.2"]]],"item:ebonlocke-s-response-to-solomon":[["item:ebonlocke-s-response-to-solomon#section2","The Crumbling Frontier","When Solomon receives the letter, the realization shakes him deeply. With the Stormwind Army withdrawn from Westfall and the royal guard absent from Darkshire, the kingdom's entire southern frontier has been left to rot. Rather than dismissing Ebonlocke's anger, Solomon recognizes a terrible pattern: the crown's sudden neglect of its outlying territories suggests treachery at the highest levels of power in Stormwind City.",["q120.5"]]],"item:fel-cone":[["item:fel-cone#section2","A Bitter Medicine","When careless travelers fall for the wiles of Zenn Foulhoof—a lurking satyr who tricks passersby into doing his bidding—local guardians demand penance. Syral Bladeleaf and the Sentinels direct remorseful adventurers to gather these noxious cones and hand them over to Foulhoof under the guise of an innocent snack. The concentrated corruption within the seed triggers a peculiar transformation, temporarily turning the foul satyr into a harmless frog.",["q488.2"]]],"item:filled-etched-phial":[["item:filled-etched-phial#section2","A Key to Centaur Sorcery","Zangen Stonehoof and Rau Cliffrunner sought this specific water due to the hidden mythic ties binding dryads and centaur. Both lineages trace their ancestry back to Cenarius, endowing their magical traditions with a deep, intertwined resonance. In Thousand Needles, the Galak clan guards an ancient relic protected by an unyielding eternal fire dating back to the creation of the centaur race. Because of their shared primal roots, the blessed moonwell water carried within this dryad phial is uniquely capable of piercing and extinguishing that protective flame.",["q1195.3"]]],"item:gem-of-anacondra":[["item:gem-of-anacondra#section2","Cleansing the Vision","Nara Wildmane and the circle of Cenarion druids in Thunder Bluff found their own slumbers plagued by the visions and wicked faces of the Fang's leadership. Claiming the Gem of Anacondra—alongside the stones of Cobrahn, Pythas, and Serpentis—is necessary to verify that Lady Anacondra has been struck down, lifting the oppressive nightmare from the minds of Kalimdor's balance-keepers.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gem-of-cobrahn":[["item:gem-of-cobrahn#section2","Restoring Balance","In Thunder Bluff, the tauren druid Nara Wildmane is tormented by nightmares sent by the rogue Druids of the Fang. To cleanse the subterranean springs and protect Kalimdor from their vision, adventurers must delve into the caverns, slay Lord Cobrahn alongside his three conspirators, and recover their dream gems. Returning Cobrahn's gem to the Cenarion druids brings an end to his corrupting influence over the slumbering Naralex.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gem-of-serpentis":[["item:gem-of-serpentis#section2","Proof for Thunder Bluff","The tauren druids atop Thunder Bluff's Elder Rise, particularly Nara Wildmane, endure vivid nightmares caused by the corruption lurking beneath the savannah. Recovering the Gem of Serpentis alongside those of Cobrahn, Anacondra, and Pythas provides tangible proof that the rogue order's ringleaders have fallen, easing the nightmare's grasp on the Cenarion Circle in Mulgore.",["q886.6"]]],"item:gnoll-paw":[["item:gnoll-paw#section2","A Coordinated Menace","Riverpaw gnolls in Westfall forage across the plains, but militia observers have noticed signs of close coordination with Defias thieves based around the Deadmines. The packs terrorize farmsteads and prey on supply wagons, helping shield the bandits from local resistance. Bringing in gnoll paws helps keep the overland routes between Sentinel Hill and Elwynn Forest open.",["defias.hideout"]]],"item:gunther-s-spellbook":[["item:gunther-s-spellbook#section1","A Token of Old Kinship","Before the Third War, Gunther Arcanus studied the arcane arts in Dalaran alongside Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw as members of the Kirin Tor. When the Plague of Undeath swept Lordaeron, Gunther fell and was raised into undeath, yet eventually managed to wrest his own mind free of the Scourge. Believing himself isolated and perpetually hounded by the Lich King's agents, he remained deeply hostile to outsiders. After the tome was recovered, Bethor Iceshard layered an arcane enchantment over its pages—a deliberate magical signature that allows Gunther to recognize Bethor's presence and realize he is not the only mage to possess free will.",["q358.4"]]],"item:head-of-vancleef":[["item:head-of-vancleef#section2","A Grim Victory","Though Stoutmantle sought VanCleef's death for the greater good of Westfall, presenting the trophy carries a heavy solemnity. VanCleef was once the revered master builder who rebuilt Stormwind after the First War, before betrayal and unpaid debts drove him and the Stonemasons Guild into outlaw rebellion. His severed head marks the culmination of Westfall's local militia campaign, yet it also exposes the kingdom's failure to handle its own domestic turmoil, leaving volunteer farmers and foreign adventurers to execute high justice.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:incendia-powder":[["item:incendia-powder#section2","Uneasy Portents","The rites dictating the creation and use of Incendia Powder stem from interpretations shared by the elder shaman Magatha Grimtotem. Believing that shifting traditions and changes in tauren ways had provoked ancient spirits into hunting High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, the powder was enchanted to force the apparition into the open so it could be destroyed. Though it succeeds in calling forth the fierce serpent, the violent encounter leaves troubled questions about why such a beast was truly stirred.",["q4821.5"]]],"item:jitters-completed-journal":[["item:jitters-completed-journal#section2","Unraveling the Darkness","The journal does more than soothe old grief: it leads Sven to reveal that he has seen the black-cloaked riders speaking with the wizard Morbent Fel at the Forlorn Rowe. Sven sends the completed manuscript with travelers north to the Cathedral of Light in Stormwind. There, Bishop Farthing reads Jitters' words to understand the vile taint festering in Duskwood, using its revelations to help craft a holy weapon capable of piercing Fel's dark wards.",["q164.5"]]],"item:letter-to-jin-zil":[["item:letter-to-jin-zil#section2","Old Memories in Malaka'jin","Upon delivery to the cave at Malaka'jin, Jin'Zil welcomes the news with nostalgic satisfaction. The death of Serena reminds the old witch doctor of the founding of Durotar after the Third War, when his friend Rokhan joined the half-ogre beastmaster Rexxar to strike down Serena's elder sister, Bloodfeather. With Serena now slain as well, Jin'Zil considers the Bloodfeather line's vengeance thoroughly extinguished.",["q867.3"]]],"item:lieutenant-s-insignia":[["item:lieutenant-s-insignia#section2","The Cleft of Shadow","Thrall directs his agent to take the token into the Cleft of Shadow and present it to the warlock Neeru Fireblade. Believing he is greeting a newly promoted subordinate, Neeru takes the bait completely. Blinded by arrogance, the warlock openly boasts that the Burning Blade operates as a direct instrument of the Shadow Council and the Burning Legion, unwittingly giving Thrall the opening he needs to expose the corruption rooted beneath the city.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]]],"item:nether-gem":[["item:nether-gem#section1","A Conduit of Arcane Communion","Forged or enchanted by Gunther Arcanus on his solitary island in Brightwater Lake, the Nether Gem serves as a mystical link across Tirisfal Glades. Gunther, having broken free from the Lich King's control through his own sheer willpower, long avoided the fledgling Forsaken nation out of suspicion. After receiving his lost spellbook, he entrusts the gem to an emissary as a gesture of goodwill. Once brought to Bethor Iceshard atop the Magic Quarter in the Undercity, the stone channels Gunther's magical visage directly before the Mage Guild leader, opening the path for the solitary mage to align with Lady Sylvanas and the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"item:nightsaber-fang":[["item:nightsaber-fang#section2","A Satyr's Reagent","Outside the settlement of Dolanaar lurks Zenn Foulhoof, a deceptive satyr who preys on eager and inexperienced travelers. Claiming a need for woodland reagents, Zenn coaxes fledgling adventurers into harvesting nightsaber fangs alongside strigid feathers and spider silk. Handing over such predatory reagents directly aids the trickster, who openly mocks the naive hero upon delivery.",["q488.2"]]],"item:pendant-of-shadow":[["item:pendant-of-shadow#section1","Token of a Dark Warlock","Held by the rogue warlock Morganth within his perch at the Tower of Ilgalar, the Pendant of Shadow is steeped in dark sorcery. Morganth sought to unravel the mysteries of the Scythe of Elune, an ancient relic of immense and volatile power. Fearing the havoc the warlock could unleash upon the Redridge Mountains and the kingdom of Stormwind, the mage Theocritus tasked adventurers with infiltrating Ilgalar, ending Morganth's plotting, and wresting the talisman from his grasp as proof of his demise.",["q94.4"]]],"item:pyrewood-shackle":[["item:pyrewood-shackle#section1","Arugal's Binding Magic","The cursed village of Pyrewood holds an unsettling secret: by day, its residents carry on ordinary lives appearing as typical humans, but the setting sun twists them into feral Moonrage worgen. Archmage Arugal bound these villagers in enchanted shackles as part of his twisted experiments with worgen sorcery. Dalar Dawnweaver, an arcane researcher stationed at the Sepulcher for the Forsaken, studies these shackles to decode the enchantments driving this dual existence. By gathering and analyzing the lingering energy on the bands, Dawnweaver seeks a deeper understanding of Arugal's magic in hopes of unraveling the mad archmage's hold over the region.",["q421.5"]]],"item:red-leather-bandana":[["item:red-leather-bandana#section1","Mark of the Brotherhood","Across Westfall’s fields and abandoned farms, highwaymen, looters, and rebellious laborers wear crimson bandanas to hide their faces. The People's Militia at Sentinel Hill sees each one as proof of loyalty to Edwin VanCleef's rogue fraternity. Scout Galiaan collects the bandanas from travelers to follow the Brotherhood’s movements and dwindling numbers across the region.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:resonite-crystal":[["item:resonite-crystal#section1","Ancient Resonance","Hidden away in the depths of Boulderslide Ravine, Resonite Crystals have become the focal point of sudden, frantic kobold excavation. While common cave miners usually seek mundane ores or shiny trinkets, the kobolds' obsession with these specific formations immediately alarmed the shamans of Sun Rock Retreat. Upon close examination, the stones hum with a subtle magical resonance tied to the primordial shaping of the world. Mor'rogal identified these faint energies as traces of Earthen magic, indicating that the burrowing creatures broke into an ancient layer crafted by the Titans.",["q6421.2"]],["item:resonite-crystal#section2","Tapping the Earth","The discovery of Resonite confirms troubling news for the Horde presence in Stonetalon: the kobold diggers have struck deep enough to disturb the ancient Earthen. Because the Earthen were shaped directly by the Titans to carve and mold the world's foundations, residual magic clings stubbornly to the surrounding rock. By gathering these crystals, Horde agents hope to decipher the kobolds' frantic intentions and turn the very power of the enchanted stone against whatever slumbering forces have been disturbed below.",["q6421.2"]]],"item:sarilus-foulborne-s-head":[["item:sarilus-foulborne-s-head#section1","A Defiler Silenced","Perched high atop Dreadmist Peak in the northern Barrens, the Forsaken spellcaster Sarilus Foulborne drew the wrath of the night elves by corrupting and binding swarms of water elementals. From her post at Silverwind Refuge, Sentinel Velene Starstrike recognized his foul handiwork and dispatched champions across the frontier into Horde territory to end his reckless experiments. Severing the mage's head was the only proof Starstrike would accept to verify that his mocking grin was wiped away forever, securing a measure of peace for the troubled waters of Ashenvale.",["q1016.2"]]],"item:sealed-description-of-thredd-s-visitor":[["item:sealed-description-of-thredd-s-visitor#section2","From Stonemasons to Spies","City architect Baros Alexston found no clear match in the description, though he suspected the visitor might be tied to the Defias rebellion. Alexston sent the sealed envelope to Master Mathias Shaw, who leads Stormwind's intelligence service, SI:7. Shaw recognized the man as a lethal mercenary who uses names including Marzon and now does dirty work for Lord Gregor Lescovar.",["q373.5"]]],"item:serena-s-head":[["item:serena-s-head#section1","A Warning on the Road","The Witchwing harpies of the Dry Hills have long plagued trade moving through the northern Barrens, but under Serena Bloodfeather their assaults turned particularly vicious. Her raids against Horde supply lines were driven by personal vengeance for her sister, who fell to the beastmaster Rexxar. To permanently break the morale of the flock, the orc rogue Darsok Swiftdagger ordered Serena hunted down. Rather than letting her death go unnoticed, he sought her severed head so it could be mounted upon the next outbound caravan—serving as a stark, bloody warning to any harpy watching from the crags above.",["q867.3"]]],"item:shaw-s-report":[["item:shaw-s-report#section1","Intelligence on the Stonemasons","Shaw's Report reaches Sentinel Hill from SI:7's covert headquarters in Stormwind. The dispatch gives the People's Militia its first clear account of who commands the bandits ravaging Westfall. Under Edwin VanCleef, the Stonemasons Guild painstakingly rebuilt Stormwind City after its destruction in the First War. When the crown and nobility refused to pay the masons fairly for their work, resentment took root. Shaw's intelligence links the displaced craftsmen to the criminal uprising now seizing the kingdom's outer territories.",["defias.masons"]]],"item:strigid-owl-feather":[["item:strigid-owl-feather#section1","Forest Plumage and Dark Dealings","Teldrassil's canopies are home to strigid owls and screechers, native birds of prey that hunt throughout the Crown of the Earth. While their down and feathers are common remnants of the forest floor, they occasionally attract the eye of practitioners seeking organic reagents. Outside Dolanaar, the renegade satyr Zenn Foulhoof exploits inexperienced travelers venturing down the road from Shadowglen, tricking them into harvesting strigid feathers alongside spider silk and nightsaber fangs for his own shady business.",["q488.2"]]],"item:the-lich-s-spellbook":[["item:the-lich-s-spellbook#section2","Unmasking Gunther Arcanus","Upon opening the glowing pages of the recovered spellbook, Bethor Iceshard made an astonishing discovery: the recluse was his old friend and comrade-in-arms from before the Third War, Gunther Arcanus. Already a prodigy in necromancy in life, Gunther's writings revealed that his arts had grown even more formidable in undeath. Bethor recognized that securing Gunther's allegiance would grant the Forsaken an invaluable master of the dark arts, provided someone could convince the suspicious lich that the Forsaken are truly free from the Lich King's grip.",["q358.4"]]],"item:thule-s-head":[["item:thule-s-head#section1","Trophy of Fenris Isle","Fenris Isle serves as the primary bastion of the Lich King's Scourge within Silverpine Forest. From deep inside Fenris Keep, the necromancer Thule Ravenclaw directs his legions of rot hides and undead minions, directly threatening Forsaken outposts like the Sepulcher. High Executor Hadrec requires absolute certainty that this dark commander has fallen, refusing to accept any testimony short of Thule's severed head. The violent release of dark magic upon Ravenclaw's death was felt across the woods, but his head remains the physical proof Hadrec requires to declare Fenris Isle broken.",["q437.6"]]],"item:translated-letter":[["item:translated-letter#section1","A Gruesome Revelation","What began as errands of mercy for a seemingly helpless hermit in Raven Hill takes a dark turn with this parchment. Written in an obscure hand, Abercrombie's message to Mayor Ello Ebonlocke remained an enigma until brought to Sirra Von'Indi for translation. The decoded text lays bare the hermit's true identity as the Embalmer. Rather than living in harmless solitude, Abercrombie used the supplies brought to him to construct a monstrous abomination named Stitches, unleashing the hulking creation to trample and slaughter the people of Darkshire.",["q165.5"]]],"item:whitestone-oak-lumber":[["item:whitestone-oak-lumber#section2","Timber of the Under-Docks","Found deep inside the labyrinthine Deadmines of Westfall, Whitestone Oak is shaped by Goblin Woodcarvers constructing the Defias Brotherhood's clandestine war fleet. Its dense, resilient grain makes it ideally suited to bear structural strain and high-impact wear, drawing the interest of seasoned armorers who require more than common ash or pine for ceremonial and martial craft.",["defias.hideout"]]],"item:wiley-s-note":[["item:wiley-s-note#section2","The Stonemason Connection","Beyond noting mercenary pacts, Wiley lets slip a crucial reference connecting the Defias syndicate to the Stonemasons Guild who rebuilt Stormwind after the Second War. Sensing the explosive political implications of this detail, Stoutmantle redirects the note directly into the hands of Master Mathias Shaw, spymaster of SI:7, pulling Stormwind's intelligence apparatus directly into the investigation.",["q123.3"]]],"item:woven-wand":[["item:woven-wand#section2","A Sundered Bond","In the days when Dalaran still trained the young magi of the north, three aspiring wizards—Bethor Iceshard, Gunther Arcanus, and Thule Ravenclaw—intertwined their individual apprentice wands into a single talisman. This Woven Wand served as both a conduit of combined magical power and a symbol of their enduring friendship. That fellowship shattered irrevocably when the Scourge swept through Lordaeron. Thule Ravenclaw chose to swear fealty to the Lich King, turning to foul necromancy. Meanwhile, the Plague of Undeath claimed both Bethor and Gunther, eventually cutting their ties to Dalaran entirely.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:abercrombie":[["npc:abercrombie#section2","The Embalmer's Madness","Before grief consumed his mind, Abercrombie was a respected, kindly alchemist living in Raven Hill. When his beloved wife, Eliza, died, sorrow broke his sanity. Turning to forbidden necromantic arts, he cut out his own heart and placed it within Eliza's dead breast to bring her back. The ritual succeeded only in creating a monster: Eliza returned cursed with an insatiable hunger for living flesh, forcing the mad alchemist to keep her buried in the cemetery.",["q165.5"]],["npc:abercrombie#section3","A Monstrous Deception","Known in dark folklore simply as 'The Embalmer,' Abercrombie nurses a deep, psychotic grudge against the township of Darkshire and its leaders. The seemingly innocuous tasks he assigns to helpful adventurers—fetching ghoul ribs, specialty thread, and caustic brews—are actually the final components required to assemble Stitches, a colossal, flesh-stitched abomination engineered to slaughter the Night Watch and ravage the town.",["q165.5"]]],"npc:arugal":[["npc:arugal#section3","The Scourge of Silverpine","From his high perch in Shadowfang Keep, Arugal extends his twisted dominion over the surrounding countryside. His dark sorcery blankets Pyrewood Village, where townsfolk live under a nocturnal curse, and infests the Deep Elem Mine with ravenous Moonrage beasts. The Forsaken now view him as an intolerable threat; Sylvanas Windrunner's Deathstalkers and apothecaries actively plot the mad archmage's execution to secure the borders of their fledgling kingdom.",["q421.5"]]],"npc:asterion":[["npc:asterion#section3","Vengeance from the Grave","Though vile satyrs and grells roam the ruins of Bashal'Aran feeding on residual arcane power, they shun the shrine where Asterion's ghost lingers. If an Alliance adventurer retrieves the moonstone seal from the satyrs and shatters it in Ameth'Aran, Asterion's spectral chains finally break. Yet freedom does not bring him peace; sensing that Athrikus Narassin still endures, the ancient mage turns his thoughts from ten thousand years of despair toward vengeance.",["q954.5"]]],"npc:baros-alexston":[["npc:baros-alexston#section2","A Bitter Parting of Ways","When Stormwind's rebuilding ended, the House of Nobles refused the agreed payment. Furious protests followed, the Stonemasons Guild dissolved, and deadly riots filled the streets. Edwin VanCleef led the unpaid artisans into exile, where they formed the Defias Brotherhood. The nobles offered Baros the post of City Architect if he disavowed the uprising. Baros disagreed with the guild's turn toward violent banditry and accepted, leaving a bitter rift between himself and his former comrades.",["defias.masons"]],["npc:baros-alexston#section3","The Ghost in the Dead Mines","Baros oversees the capital's upkeep from his desk in City Hall, and Mathias Shaw of SI:7 discreetly owes him a favor. After VanCleef fell in the depths of the Deadmines, an unsent letter addressed to Baros remained on the Defias leader's corpse. Its presence recalls the bond broken by politics, treason, and bloodshed over Stormwind's foundations.",["defias.hideout"]]],"npc:bethor-iceshard":[["npc:bethor-iceshard#section2","Old Friends and Bittersweet Reunions","Bethor has not forgotten his former companions. Tracking down powerful undead spellcasters throughout Tirisfal and Silverpine, he dispatches agents to discern whether rogue practitioners serve the Lich King or might be turned toward the Forsaken cause. When Bethor identifies Gunther Arcanus raising the dead on Brightwater Lake, he works patiently to bring his old friend into Lady Sylvanas's fold, while offering no mercy to Thule Ravenclaw, whose willing betrayal doomed their past brotherhood.",["q358.4"]],["npc:bethor-iceshard#section3","Bonds of Apprenticehood","Before the Third War devastated Lordaeron, Bethor was a mortal apprentice studying magic alongside two close companions: Gunther Arcanus and Thule Ravenclaw. As a testament to their fellowship, the three students wove their practice wands together. The Scourge shattered that bond entirely. When death and dark magic swept across the kingdom, Thule Ravenclaw willfully surrendered his soul to the Lich King, Gunther perished and rose on an isolated island in Brightwater Lake, and Bethor succumbed only to later break free as Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:brainwashed-noble":[["npc:brainwashed-noble#section1","A Pawn of the Brotherhood","Brainwashed Noble wanders the passages leading into the Deadmines, among bandits and rotting corpses. Remnants of highborn finery suggest he belonged to Stormwind's House of Nobles, perhaps as a sitting member or a scion. Edwin VanCleef took him captive, and the Defias chose enchantments over ransom, bending his mind into that of a spellcasting guard for their sanctuary. The Brotherhood's hatred of the peers who refused to pay after the city was rebuilt reaches even into the caverns beneath Westfall.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"npc:chef-grual":[["npc:chef-grual#section2","Secret Ingredients","Grual is infamous for his specialty dishes, most notably his famous 'Dusky Crab Cakes' and seasoned wolf kabobs. Given that coastal crabs are nowhere near the gloomy inland woods of Duskwood, his crab cakes rely on a rather revolting substitute: the gooey legs of local Venom Web Spiders. Whether patrons realize they are eating arachnid meat or simply prefer not to ask, Grual keeps the kitchen running without apologies.",["q5.2"]]],"npc:deathstalker-faerleia":[["npc:deathstalker-faerleia#section1","Behind Enemy Lines","As an agent of the Deathstalkers—the covert intelligence and assassin branch of the Undercity—Faerleia is tasked with infiltrating Pyrewood Village in southern Silverpine Forest. The isolated human enclave harbors dark secrets, including a curse that transforms its populace under the night sky. Stationed within the upper levels of the town hall, Faerleia coordinates operations to disrupt the settlement from the inside, calling on Horde operatives to eliminate the influential members of the Pyrewood Council.",["q421.5"]]],"npc:ebru":[["npc:ebru#section2","The Price of Corruption","The nightmare twisted not only the ecosystem of the caves, but also Ebru's fellow disciples, turning them into the fanatical Druids of the Fang. Stripped of her companionship and facing the ruin of her circle, Ebru focuses on containment.",["q886.5","q886.6"]]],"npc:edwin-vancleef":[["npc:edwin-vancleef#section3","Retribution from the Shadows","Taking advantage of the crown's neglect of the countryside, the Defias drove off Westfall's farmers and claimed its rich gold deposits. Within the depths of Moonbrook's Deadmines, VanCleef oversaw the construction of a massive juggernaut warship, preparing an armed assault against Stormwind itself. While the House of Nobles dismissed the thieves as disorganized brigands, VanCleef forged alliances with river crooks, gnolls, and pirates, turning an embittered labor union into an army poised to crush the monarchy.",["defias.juggernaut"]]],"npc:foreman-thistlenettle":[["npc:foreman-thistlenettle#section2","Tragedy in the Deadmines","Before the Defias Brotherhood took full control of the Deadmines, mining crews worked its rich veins. Foreman Thistlenettle led one such expedition for the Miners' League and the Explorers' League. Deep beneath Moonbrook, a violent cave-in sealed his party away from the surface. The miners died in the darkness, but did not remain at rest. A necrotic taint raised Thistlenettle and his crew as ravenous ghouls, stalking the shadowed passages.",["defias.hideout"]]],"npc:gelkak-gyromast":[["npc:gelkak-gyromast#section2","The Threshwackonator Malfunction","Once adventurers recover the key, Gyromast sends them to switch on his first mate and lead it back to him, eager to set it loose on the murlocs and beasts of the coast. The Threshwackonator 4100 follows obediently, only to malfunction and turn hostile the moment it reaches its captain, leaving the adventurers to destroy it.",["q2098.3"]]],"npc:glorin-steelbrow":[["npc:glorin-steelbrow#section2","The Doomed Fleet and Lost Relics","Steelbrow is intimately familiar with the tragedy of the Third Fleet, whose ships were battered and dragged beneath the waves along the Wetlands shore. He guides adventurers to scour the haunted wreckage for lost ingots of Lightforge iron and the powerful, cursed artifact known as the Eye of Paleth. Glorin knows that recovering such holy armaments is vital for forging weapons capable of holding back the dark forces threatening the Alliance.",["q288.4"]]],"npc:gryan-stoutmantle":[["npc:gryan-stoutmantle#section3","Unmasking the Kingpin","Gryan Stoutmantle sought to learn who was directing Westfall's ruin. He called in an old debt from Wiley the Black, a rogue in Redridge, and conferred with Mathias Shaw of SI:7. Their intelligence revealed that Edwin VanCleef led the bandits. VanCleef was the slighted master architect of the Stonemasons' Guild. After the militia captured a bandit trying to plunder a local wagon, Gryan used the prisoner to find the Brotherhood's headquarters beneath Moonbrook, within the Deadmines. He sent veteran champions to strike VanCleef directly.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"npc:gunther-arcanus":[["npc:gunther-arcanus#section2","The Prodigal Lich","Seeking powerful allies against the Scourge, the Forsaken sent emissaries to claim the mysterious necromancer's spellbook. Upon examining the arcane tome in the Undercity, Bethor Iceshard recognized the unique handwriting of his long-lost peer. By enchanting the returned grimoire and demanding the destruction of local Scourge commanders, Gunther finally realized he was not alone in his freedom. Convinced that Sylvanas's faction represents a true rebellion against the Scourge, he has forged a pact with his former schoolmate to aid the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]],["npc:gunther-arcanus#section3","A Student of Dalaran","Before the fall of Lordaeron, Gunther was a promising apprentice among the Kirin Tor in Dalaran. Alongside close friends Bethor Iceshard and Thule Ravenclaw, he studied the arcane arts, secretly delving into necromancy despite the Kirin Tor's strict prohibitions against dark magic. To commemorate their brotherhood, the trio once bound their apprentice wands together into a single heirloom.",["q358.4"]]],"npc:kam-deepfury":[["npc:kam-deepfury#section3","A Score to Settle","The Dark Iron insurrectionist did not manage to escape retribution for long, though imprisonment in Stormwind offered him temporary protection from his victims' relatives. Back in the Wetlands, Motley Garmason and the grieving Longbraid the Grim have sought mercenaries willing to infiltrate the riot-torn prison and sever Deepfury's head, settling a bloody grudge sparked hundreds of miles to the north.",["q303.3"]]],"npc:lord-serpentis":[["npc:lord-serpentis#section3","The Final Obstacle","Perched deep inside the winding caverns, Lord Serpentis is one of the four leaders of the rogue sect, each of whom carries a dream gem. Those seeking to wake the trapped master or reclaim the waters of the Oasis must strike down Serpentis and claim the gem he carries, breaking the serpent's hold over the labyrinth.",["q886.6"]]],"npc:mathias-shaw":[["npc:mathias-shaw#section2","Shadow of VanCleef","Mathias Shaw once called Edwin VanCleef a childhood companion. Shaw trained VanCleef in stealth and assassination, hoping the gifted stonemason would serve the kingdom beside him. But after the Stonemasons Guild was cast out following Stormwind's rebuilding, VanCleef turned those lessons against the crown. Shaw now directs surveillance and infiltration across Westfall to break his old friend's rebellion.",["defias.masons"]]],"npc:morgan-the-collector":[["npc:morgan-the-collector#section2","A Signet of the Past","Morgan carries a distinctive signet ring that marks his authority. Investigators in Goldshire see the ring as evidence connecting the masked thieves terrorizing the heartland to the disgraced Stonemasons Guild, which rebuilt Stormwind City after the Second War. Recovering the ring reveals who directs the bandits in the woods.",["q123.3"]]],"npc:naralex":[["npc:naralex#section3","Fallen Disciples","The nightmare did not stop with the wildlife. Naralex's own disciples, driven completely mad by the psychic backlash bleeding out of their master's trance, broke away from the traditions of the Cenarion Circle. Casting aside their reverence for balance, they took on serpent forms and renamed themselves the Druids of the Fang. Believing their twisted growth must spread across Kalimdor, they now guard Naralex's slumbering body to ensure he never wakes, leaving only a few loyal disciples searching for allies brave enough to break the trance.",["q886.5"]]],"npc:orgnil-soulscar":[["npc:orgnil-soulscar#section2","Hunting the Shadow Council's Remnants","Orgnil's chief focus is the clandestine resurgence of the Burning Blade cult, whose warlocks hide in caves such as Skull Rock. Knowing the devastation fel magic brought upon the orcish people in past generations, Orgnil refuses to let demon worship gain another foothold near Orgrimmar. He directs trusted recruits to strike down cultists, gather ritual talismans, and consult with local hermits and shamans like Margoz to understand the dark magics at play.",["q5726.3"]]],"npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden":[["npc:rethiel-the-greenwarden#section2","Guardian of the Marsh","Standing amidst the damp thickets of the Green Belt, Rethiel is a towering creature of moss, peat, and primal plant life. While many bog beasts in the marshlands wander aimlessly as aggressive predators, Rethiel possesses a rare, conscious awareness and a sorrowful voice. He perceives the wetlands not merely as murky terrain, but as a living ecosystem that currently cries out under the weight of foreign predation and corruption.",["q463.2"]]],"npc:sven-yorgen":[["npc:sven-yorgen#section3","Uncovering the Truth","The shadowy figure stalking Sven's property turns out to be Jitters, an errant vagrant whose blundering inadvertently drew the Dark Riders into Duskwood in their search for the Scythe of Elune. Though Jitters did not strike the fatal blows, his actions unchained the nightmare that obliterated the Yorgen family, directing Sven's fury toward the dark forces haunting Raven Hill and the necromancer Morbent Fel.",["q164.5"]]],"npc:the-defias-traitor":[["npc:the-defias-traitor#section1","Leading the Way to Moonbrook","Under guard at Sentinel Hill's watchtower, the traitor must lead Alliance champions through hostile territory to the hidden entrance beneath ruined Moonbrook. His former comrades hate him, and his captors despise him. Yet his betrayal gives the People's Militia the opening it needs to strike at the Brotherhood's heart.",["defias.hideout"]],["npc:the-defias-traitor#section2","A Bargain for Survival","The Defias Brotherhood does not command every member's loyalty to Edwin VanCleef. Gryan Stoutmantle's militia caught the petty thief trying to steal Farmer Saldean's wagon. Facing execution, he chose to save himself. He gave the militia the exact location of the Brotherhood's hidden base in exchange for his life.",["defias.masons"]]],"npc:verdan-the-everliving":[["npc:verdan-the-everliving#section2","Twisted by the Nightmare","When Naralex attempted to cultivate the dry Barrens into a lush paradise, his ritual fractured into a vivid nightmare within the Emerald Dream. The resulting corruption bled through the subterranean waters, tainting everything living inside the cavern. Amid that blight, Verdan roams as an enormous, hostile brute in the depths shared with the mad Druids of the Fang.",["q886.5"]]],"npc:zenn-foulhoof":[["npc:zenn-foulhoof#section1","A Schemer in the Woods","Unlike most satyrs, who are outright hostiles serving demonic masters, Zenn Foulhoof somehow secured a tenuous tolerance just outside Dolanaar. He maintains a friendly facade, claiming he has changed or wants to trade in harmless forest reagents like nightsaber fangs, strigid owl feathers, and webwood spider silk. In reality, he manipulates gullible youths to procure ingredients for foul rituals. Dolanaar's council refuses to let him operate unchecked; rather than executing him immediately, they order adventurers to humiliate him by feeding him corrupted forest fruit, temporarily transforming the scheming satyr into a harmless frog.",["q488.2"]]],"subzone:ambermill":[["subzone:ambermill#section2","Tapping the Ley Lines","The Kirin Tor's interest in Ambermill extends far beyond simple territorial defense. The village rests directly atop a dormant ley line energy node. Forsaken spies and Deathstalkers monitoring the town have discovered that the Dalaran magi are attempting to reactivate this nexus for large-scale arcane work. If completed, the surge of magical power could threaten the Forsaken's fragile hold on Silverpine Forest, prompting Shadow Priest Allister and the Deathstalkers to orchestrate sabotage, thefts of Kirin Tor documents, and assassinations across the mill grounds.",["q477.3"]]],"subzone:ameth-aran":[["subzone:ameth-aran#section2","Betrayal and Eternal Torment","As the world began to tear itself apart, Athrikus Narassin returned to Ameth'Aran, promising his panicked subjects salvation through powerful shielding magic. Instead of shielding them, the sorcerer enacted a horrific betrayal, stealing their life force to feed his own soul gems under the cruel guise of giving them eternal preservation. Shattered by cataclysm and dark magic, Ameth'Aran fell. Today, its grounds are stalked by anguished Highborne specters, unable to find rest ten thousand years after their murder.",["q965.3"]]],"subzone:boulderslide-cavern":[["subzone:boulderslide-cavern#section2","The Slumbering Giant","Deeper inside the caverns, reckless excavation has breached ancient secrets. Embedded in the subterranean rock lies a resonite cask containing Goggeroc, a slumbering earthen from ages past. The kobolds' relentless digging around this mystical relic risks awakening a being imbued with primordial stone magic, posing a sudden danger to the entire ravine.",["q6421.2"]]],"subzone:brightwater-lake":[["subzone:brightwater-lake#section2","The Hermit's Refuge","Rising from the center of Brightwater Lake is an island known as Gunther's Retreat. Gunther Arcanus broke free of the Lich King's control on his own, but believed all other undead remained Scourge slaves. He isolated himself and set undead servants to guard his retreat. Bethor Iceshard seeks to show his old friend that the Forsaken also possess free will. After an adventurer proves their opposition to the Scourge, Gunther agrees to join the Forsaken.",["q358.4"]]],"subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle":[["subzone:darkcloud-pinnacle#section2","Schemes on the Mesas","Commanded by leaders such as Arnak Grimtotem and Harb Foulmountain, the settlement is more than a simple outpost. Secret correspondence discovered on the mesa suggests the Grimtotem are actively coordinating with dark and unknown powers, pursuing ambitions far removed from the peace forged by Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff.",["q4542.4"]]],"subzone:dead-field":[["subzone:dead-field#section2","Ravenclaw's Ambition","The Rot Hide gnolls do not act alone. Their operations in the Dead Field are orchestrated from afar by the necromancer Thule Ravenclaw, based out of Fenris Isle. Intending to assert Scourge dominance over Silverpine and drive Sylvanas's forces out of Lordaeron, Ravenclaw projects magical apparitions to the farmstead to survey his undead horde and issue warnings to Forsaken intruders.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:deadmines":[["subzone:deadmines#section2","VanCleef's Foundry","After Stormwind's stonemasons rioted, the newly formed Defias Brotherhood took the Deadmines as its chief refuge. Stormwind's Miners' League tried to reopen the tunnels under Foreman Thistlenettle, but Defias brigands ambushed the workers and collapsed the shafts. The slain miners now wander as restless undead. Ogre enforcers and goblin tinkerers guard the deep tunnels, where the Defias have built an industrial war camp around a shipyard in Ironclad Cove.",["defias.masons"]]],"subzone:defias-hideout":[["subzone:defias-hideout#section1","A Gateway in Ruin","The Defias Hideout hides Edwin VanCleef’s southern operations beneath a ruined house in Moonbrook. Vagrants and Defias thugs move through its ransacked rooms, much like those of any other manor or farmhouse in the settlement. On the upper floor, an opening leads down into the winding passages of the Deadmines. The Brotherhood keeps most of its strength around Moonbrook’s perimeter and at work underground, so few guards remain inside. They watch the hideout as a narrow passage into the mines, not as a stronghold of its own.",["defias.hideout"]]],"subzone:dreadmist-peak":[["subzone:dreadmist-peak#section2","A Convergence of Schemes","Beyond housing warlocks and their dark rituals around Dreadmist Den, the peak has drawn another malevolent figure. The rogue Forsaken mage Sarilus Foulborne made his camp upon the heights, bending elemental forces to corrupt water spirits as far north as Ashenvale.",["q1016.2"]]],"subzone:fenris-isle":[["subzone:fenris-isle#section2","The Master of Fenris Keep","The Rot Hide gnolls do not act alone. Within the crumbling stone halls of Fenris Keep dwells Thule Ravenclaw, a cunning necromancer formerly aligned with the Kirin Tor who turned to the dark arts of the Scourge. Thule bent the native gnolls to his will, using dark sorcery to animate their fallen kin and fortify the island against intrusion. From this isolated sanctuary, Thule oversees the lake's ferry crossings, prompting Forsaken scouts and Horde forces to launch preemptive strikes to break his hold on the archipelago.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:fenris-keep":[["subzone:fenris-keep#section1","The Scourge on Lordamere","Today, Fenris Keep serves as the seat of Ravenclaw's power and an imminent threat to the Forsaken securing Silverpine Forest. From this bastion, Thule directs legions of Rot Hide gnolls that violently assail anyone daring to set foot on the isle. Because Fenris Isle sits mere miles across the water from the Tirisfal border, the Forsaken of the Sepulcher and Undercity actively dispatch champions to batter down the keep's defenses and put an end to the rogue necromancer.",["q437.6"]],["subzone:fenris-keep#section2","A Fallen Bastion of Lordaeron","Before the Third War, Fenris Keep held strategic importance for the Kingdom of Lordaeron, stationing a sizable contingent of troops to safeguard Lordamere Lake. When the undead Scourge swept across the land, the island fortress was overwhelmed along with the rest of the realm. Rather than crumbling into forgotten ruin, it was seized by Thule Ravenclaw, a traitorous former wizard of the Kirin Tor. Ravenclaw allied himself with the Scourge, subjugated the resident gnolls with foul necromancy to create the ravenous Rot Hide pack, and entrenched himself deep inside the keep's stone halls.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:lordamere-lake":[["subzone:lordamere-lake#section2","A Contested Expanse","Today, navigating the dark waters of Lordamere is fraught with peril. At the center of the lake sits Fenris Isle, where the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw and his Rot Hide gnolls hold out against the Forsaken. Further east lie the Dawning Isles, completely overrun by aggressive Vile Fin murlocs. From the ruins around Dalaran to the shoreline of the Undercity, the lake is no longer a peaceful highway, but a drowned frontier contested by remnant monsters and the armies of the dead.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:lost-fleet":[["subzone:lost-fleet#section2","The Curse of Paleth","A watery grave was not the end for the sailors of the Third Fleet. Locked deep in the sunken cargo hold of the Intrepid lay a forbidden artifact known as the Cursed Eye of Paleth, hoarded by the ship's greedy commander, Captain Halyndor. The dark relic corrupted the drowned crews, binding their spirits to the splintered timbers as raving, cursed undead. Only the ghost of First Mate Snellig retains any semblance of sanity, wandering the marshy shore while his former crewmates violently lash out at any trespassers who venture near the wrecks.",["q288.4"]]],"subzone:lost-point":[["subzone:lost-point#section2","Den of Deserters","The deserters ensconced at Lost Point answer to Balos Jacken, a disgruntled former soldier who has banded these rogues together. Their presence is far more than a simple matter of military insubordination. Jacken and his cohorts hold key testimonies regarding traitorous elements within Theramore's ranks, including the involvement of Private Hendel in a high-stakes conspiracy that reaches into the upper echelons of the Alliance.",["q1274.5"]]],"subzone:mannoroc-coven":[["subzone:mannoroc-coven#section1","A Bastion of Fel Fire","Situated south of Ghost Walker Post and east of Shadowprey Village, Mannoroc Coven blankets the ancient night elf ruins in an oppressive, fel-tainted pall. The coven is commanded on the ground by Lord Azrethoc, alongside the Burning Blade warlock Jugkar Grim'rod. Together, they harness active summoning portals to pull demonic reinforcements straight into Kalimdor, including doomguards, infernals, and felhounds. Even as Horde-aligned travelers attempt to sever these links using relics like the Hand of Iruxos, new rifts continue to tear open through the scarred earth.",["q1480.4"]]],"subzone:manor-mistmantle":[["subzone:manor-mistmantle#section2","The Master of the Estate","The manor belongs to Stalvan Mistmantle, a former schoolteacher and private tutor whose horrific crimes against the family of his student Tilloa became the stuff of dark legend across the kingdom. Now an agent of the undead, Stalvan lurks in the upper floor of his family home, surrounded by remnants of his slaughtered victims and guarded by shambling horrors. Travelers and investigators from Darkshire frequently find that the trail of Duskwood's most notorious phantom ends at this blood-soaked manor.",["q66.7"]]],"subzone:moonbrook":[["subzone:moonbrook#section2","Defias Stronghold","Moonbrook’s streets and ransacked homes are patrolled by red-masked thugs and rogue wizards. Harvest golems roam the fields around town. At the edge of Moonbrook, an unassuming barn hides an entrance to the Deadmines beneath it. The Brotherhood guards the town above its tunnels, where rebellion against Stormwind is being prepared.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"subzone:olsen-s-farthing":[["subzone:olsen-s-farthing#section2","The Grasp of Ravenclaw","The shambling horrors haunting Olsen's Farthing are not mindless wanderers, but servants answering to Thule Ravenclaw, a powerful necromancer operating from Fenris Isle in the center of Lake Lordamere. Using dark sorcery to animate and enslave the dead of the surrounding countryside, Thule's reach extends through Olsen's Farthing and poses a direct threat to the Forsaken seeking to secure the northern reaches of Silverpine Forest.",["q437.6"]]],"subzone:pyrewood-village":[["subzone:pyrewood-village#section2","The Moonrage Curse","From the towers of Shadowfang Keep, Archmage Arugal unleashed otherworldly beasts upon the surrounding lands, ultimately cursing the living population of Pyrewood. By the light of day, the citizens appear as normal human villagers, quietly maintaining their town hall, blacksmith, and chapel, even offering trade to visiting Alliance travelers. Yet as soon as the sun dips beneath the horizon, a terrible transformation occurs: every resident shifts into a savage, bloodthirsty worgen of the Moonrage pack, turning on anyone unfortunate enough to linger within the walls.",["q421.5"]]],"subzone:ragefire-chasm":[["subzone:ragefire-chasm#section2","The Searing Blade","Far more dangerous than the troggs is the Searing Blade, an insurgent sect of the demonic Shadow Council operating from the shadows of the chasm. Directed by the warlock Jergosh the Invoker, the satyr Bazzalan, and a towering felguard known as Taragaman the Hungerer, these cultists plot the ruin of Thrall's Horde. Though Neeru Fireblade claims to oppose them, the cult works actively to test their forces and sabotage the orcs' new home from below.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:ragefire-chasm-classic":[["subzone:ragefire-chasm-classic#section2","The Searing Blade","Far more dangerous than mindless troggs is the presence of the Searing Blade, an insurgent cult subordinate to the demonic Shadow Council. Led by the hulking felguard Taragaman the Hungerer, the orc warlock Jergosh the Invoker, and the corrupt satyr Bazzalan, these conspirators scheme to unravel everything Thrall's Horde has established in Durotar. Rather than immediately cleansing the caves, Warchief Thrall has maintained surveillance on their activities, hoping their foolish overconfidence will expose their masters higher up in the Shadow Council's hierarchy.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:roland-s-doom":[["subzone:roland-s-doom#section2","The Fall of Velinde Starsong","The true tragedy of Roland's Doom lies buried deep within its tunnels. It was here that the night elven priestess Velinde Starsong met her end while carrying the Scythe of Elune, an ancient artifact of immense and volatile power. Disturbed dirt within the mine can still stir the lingering spirit of Velinde to speak of her final moments. Later, the terrified wanderer Jitters stumbled upon the scythe inside the delve, accidentally unleashing an escalation of savage worgen and drawing the deadly Dark Riders to Duskwood.",["q1022.6"]]],"subzone:thandol-span":[["subzone:thandol-span#section2","A Lifeline Severed","Constructed following the War of the Three Hammers to bridge Ironforge with the Wildhammer clan of the Aerie Peak, the Thandol Span stands among the grandest stonecraft feats of dwarven history. Capable of supporting entire marching armies, it served as the critical land corridor between Lordaeron and Khaz Modan throughout the Second War, notably carrying Sir Anduin Lothar's counter-offensive southward. Today, the crossing is grievously wounded. Dark Iron saboteurs led by Balgaras the Foul and Kam Deepfury mounted a devastating assault from nearby Dun Modr, shattering one span entirely with explosives and threatening to sever overland travel between the allied realms completely.",["q303.3"]]],"subzone:tower-of-althalaxx":[["subzone:tower-of-althalaxx#section2","The Dark Strand","In the modern era, the tower has stirred with malignant sorcery. Warlocks, dark cultists calling themselves the Cult of the Dark Strand, and allied satyrs have congregated within the ruin under the leadership of Athrikus Narassin himself. Their rites channel demonic magic and torment the lingering spirits of the ancient dead, serving sinister designs tied directly to the demonic Shadow Council.",["q965.3"]]],"subzone:valley-of-trials":[["subzone:valley-of-trials#section2","The Shadow in the North","What was once an isolated training ground has recently taken on a far more sinister edge. In the natural caves along the northern cliffs, cultists of the Burning Blade have taken root, summoning minor demonic familiars into the valley. While trainers treat these interlopers as live-combat practice for fresh recruits, the presence of fel magic so close to Orgrimmar's threshold fuels dark rumors that the Shadow Council is actively attempting to sink its claws back into the orcish people.",["q5726.3"]]],"subzone:wailing-caverns":[["subzone:wailing-caverns#section1","The Druids of the Fang","As Naralex's slumber twisted into madness, his closest disciples were driven insane by the intrusive Nightmare. Renouncing their ancient traditions, they forged the Druids of the Fang under leaders like Lady Anacondra, embracing venomous snake aspects and defending their sleeping master from any who would break the trance. The subterranean waterways now threaten to poison the oases throughout the Barrens, drawing the attention of both concerned Cenarion Circle allies in Thunder Bluff and opportunistic goblins in Ratchet.",["q886.5","q886.6"]],["subzone:wailing-caverns#section2","The Dream Curdled","The caverns earn their name from the eerie, mournful wails that escape rock fissures when natural underground geysers erupt into steam. The night elf druid Naralex ventured into these deep waterways with his faithful followers, hoping to tap into the subterranean network and channel the Emerald Dream to make the barren savannas bloom. Instead, Naralex's consciousness was trapped within the Emerald Nightmare. The festering corruption bleeds outward through the slumbering druid, warping the springs and turning local beasts into unnatural deviate horrors.",["q886.5"]]],"topic:burning-blade-clan":[["topic:burning-blade-clan#section3","The Traitor in the Cleft","While Thrall works to establish the New Horde, the cult's master orchestrator sits directly under his nose. Neeru Fireblade, an ostensible Horde warlock dwelling in the Cleft of Shadow, secretly leads the Burning Blade as an agent of the Shadow Council. Neeru uses pawns, including the expendable Searing Blade of Ragefire Chasm, to maintain his disguise as a benign researcher helping the Warchief. Unbeknownst to Neeru, Thrall has already deduced his treacherous allegiance and watches him closely.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]],["topic:burning-blade-clan#section4","A Split Legacy","Following the Second War, the surviving remnants of the Burning Blade split dramatically. A disciplined contingent of blademasters swore solemn oaths to liberate their people from demonic enslavement, pledging their blades directly to Warchief Thrall as his personal honor guard. Yet a far larger portion of the scattered clan fell entirely under the sway of the Burning Legion and the Shadow Council, morphing into a clandestine cult of warlocks, fel acolytes, and conspirators lurking throughout Kalimdor.",["q5726.3"]]],"topic:dark-riders":[["topic:dark-riders#section3","Shadows over Duskwood","Whispers in Duskwood link the Riders' sinister hunts to the rogue necromancer Morbent Fel, though no one knows what binds the two together, or who the 'Lord' the Riders answer to may be.",["q164.5"]]],"topic:defias-brotherhood":[["topic:defias-brotherhood#section1","A Web of Conspiracies","The Brotherhood has long outgrown a simple band of displaced laborers. VanCleef recruited mercenary goblins to oversee armaments, struck alliances with Riverpaw gnolls and Elwynn kobolds, and traded with cutthroat pirates like the Bloodsail Buccaneers. Defias highwaymen raid gold shipments across Elwynn Forest, and their agents even established a foothold in Duskwood before unearthing a cursed artifact in Roland's Doom. Far worse, the Brotherhood's influence reaches into the very heart of the capital, enjoying covert support from treacherous aristocrats within the House of Nobles while orchestrating kidnappings that strike at the highest offices of the Alliance.",["unsent.lescovar"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section2","The Juggernaught in the Deadmines","Beneath Moonbrook lies the Ironclad Cove, where VanCleef's goblin engineers are secretly assembling an enormous iron-plated warship known as the Defias Juggernaught. The guildmaster intends to sail this floating fortress out through the subterranean coastal waterways and bombard Stormwind City from the sea, reducing his own grand architecture to rubble. The conspiracy extends into the royal court itself: corrupt nobles like Lord Gregor Lescovar serve as eyes and ears for the uprising, while the court machinations of Lady Katrana Prestor deliberately keep the kingdom fractured and blind to the rising fleet beneath Westfall.",["defias.juggernaut","unsent.lescovar","prestor.onyxia"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section3","From Masons to Marauders","Following the Second War, the Stonemasons Guild was hired to rebuild Stormwind from smoldering ruins into a grand stone capital. When their masterwork was finished, the House of Nobles refused to deliver the promised payment, citing crushing wartime debt. Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef spoke out on behalf of his workers, demanding fair restitution, but the nobles responded by ordering the Stonemasons Guild disbanded. The ensuing riots in the city streets ended with the laborers driven out of Stormwind into exile. Stripped of their livelihood and outlawed, VanCleef and the desperate workers banded together, binding their faces in red silk to represent the blood of the wronged, and turned to highway robbery to claim what they believed they were owed.",["defias.masons"]],["topic:defias-brotherhood#section4","The Shadow Over Westfall","With the royal army overextended, VanCleef turned the neglected farmlands of Westfall into the Brotherhood's base of operations. The bandits overran the hamlet of Moonbrook and commandeered the abandoned subterranean tunnels of the Deadmines. Defias agents seized local mines, driven farmers from their homesteads with reprogrammed harvest golems, and salted the earth to starve the region. With the Crown unable or unwilling to send soldiers to protect rural citizens, local farmers formed The People's Militia out of Sentinel Hill to fight a desperate war of attrition against an enemy that now outmans and outguns them.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:druids-of-the-fang":[["topic:druids-of-the-fang#section2","Descent into Nightmare","Something went horribly awry inside Naralex's trance. Instead of channeling the pristine serenity of the Emerald Dream, Naralex’s slumber was infiltrated by a malevolent, twisting nightmare. This darkness seeped outwards through the caverns, rapidly warping the resident wildlife and poisoning the underground springs. Rather than fleeing to seek aid from the Cenarion Circle, Naralex's acolytes were overwhelmed by the nightmare's influence, their minds and spirits mutating alongside the subterranean ecosystem.",["q886.5"]]],"topic:gnoll":[["topic:gnoll#section4","Pestilence and Territory","Gnoll menace is not limited to the human south. In northern Lordaeron, the Scourge necromancer Thule Ravenclaw raises undead Rot Hide gnolls to dig up corpses across Tirisfal and Silverpine. Near Hillsbrad, the Mudsnout harvest foul, toxic blossoms among ruined farmsteads. Across the Great Sea in Kalimdor, the Palemane pack butchers game across Mulgore despite tauren efforts to make peace, while the Woodpaw of Feralas wage a desperate, frenzied war against encroaching silithid swarms.",["q437.6"]]],"topic:goblin":[["topic:goblin#section3","Hired Hands and Industry","A goblin's primary loyalty belongs to gold, and many operate outside the neutral cartels as hired specialists or ruthless corporate barons. The Venture Company pursues unchecked deforestation and resource stripping across continents, heedless of local populations. Elsewhere, skilled artificers sell their talents directly to criminal enterprises: deep inside the Deadmines, goblin engineers like Sneed, Gilnid, and Captain Greenskin oversee smelters and shipwrights constructing a massive ironclad juggernaut for the Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:grimtotem-tribe":[["topic:grimtotem-tribe#section3","Shadows and Apothecaries","Beneath Magatha's veneer of spiritual counsel sits an intricate web of intrigue. The Elder Crone is an ardent sponsor of the tauren's controversial ties with the Forsaken, publicly claiming she wishes to heal the undead, while secretly seeking out toxic plagues and Highborne artifacts. In Thousand Needles, scouts from Freewind Post intercept guarded correspondence between cloaked couriers and Grimtotem chieftains in Darkcloud Pinnacle.",["q4542.4"]]],"topic:house-of-nobles":[["topic:house-of-nobles#section3","Whispers of Treason","The council's obstinacy is far from simple greed. In truth, Lady Katrana Prestor—secretly the black dragon Onyxia—has systematically manipulated the House of Nobles to paralyze the kingdom. By stoking the aristocracy's arrogance during the stonemason negotiations and encouraging them to hoard royal funds, she orchestrated the realm's internal paralysis. High-ranking aristocrats like Lord Gregor Lescovar actively conspire alongside the Defias Brotherhood in the shadow of the throne, ensuring no imperial troops march to save the outlying towns.",["q65.7"]],["topic:house-of-nobles#section4","The Labor Revolt","Following the Second War, the House of Nobles contracted Guildmaster Edwin VanCleef and the Stonemasons Guild to rebuild Stormwind City from its ashes. However, the nobility simultaneously incurred staggering debts while expanding the kingdom's military frontier south into Stranglethorn. When the gleaming capital was finished, the nobles balked at the invoice and refused to pay the workers their full wages. Enraged by the betrayal, the masons rioted in the streets, prompting the nobility to banish the guild, driving the ruined workers into Westfall to form the Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.masons"]]],"topic:juggernaught":[["topic:juggernaught#section2","Ironclad Cove","Hidden beneath the hills of Westfall, deep within the subterranean caverns of the Deadmines, lies Ironclad Cove. There, secluded away from prying eyes and royal inspectors, Edwin VanCleef's craftsmen labored secretly to construct a fully armed juggernaught. By assembling a heavy warship in an underground cavern connected directly to the open sea, the Defias planned to bypass Stormwind's overland defenses and launch a surprise naval strike right into the heart of the kingdom.",["defias.hideout"]]],"topic:stonemasons-guild":[["topic:stonemasons-guild#section3","Blood in the Streets","The wage dispute erupted into violent unrest throughout Stormwind. The Crown cracked down ruthlessly, capturing key conspirators like VanCleef's lieutenant, Bazil Thredd, while VanCleef and hundreds of ruined stonemasons fled into the countryside of Westfall. Exiled and branded as traitors, the desperate artisans transformed their trade union into the militant Defias Brotherhood.",["defias.masons"]]],"topic:stormwind-army":[["topic:stormwind-army#section3","A Thin Blue Line","Despite its proud traditions, the modern army is stretched beyond its breaking point. Debts incurred by the House of Nobles to expand military reach into Stranglethorn fueled the bitter rift with the Stonemasons Guild, culminating in civil riots and the birth of the Defias Brotherhood. With King Varian missing, the House of Nobles has routinely withheld troops, funding, and supplies from outlying settlements like Westfall, Lakeshire, and Duskwood, forcing local marshals to deputize wandering adventurers to carry the realm's burdens.",["q123.3"]]],"topic:westfall-brigade":[["topic:westfall-brigade#section3","Uncovering the Conspiracy","The People's Militia learns the scale of the Defias threat through scouting and outside help. The Brotherhood is more than a band of country thieves: it operates under Edwin VanCleef, the former Guildmaster of the Stonemasons who rebuilt Stormwind. From the Deadmines beneath Moonbrook, the Defias plan to overthrow the kingdom. The embattled Militia is the only barrier between the capital and open insurrection.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout"]]],"zone:barrens":[["zone:barrens#section3","The Emerald Corruption","Several oases punctuate the northern plains, their waters unnaturally surging with life-giving vigor. This surge originates from the Wailing Caverns, where the night elf druid Naralex attempted to tap into the Emerald Dream to restore the Barrens to its ancient, forested glory. Instead, a nightmare twisted Naralex's vision. Corrupted water now seeps across the Forgotten Pools and Stagnant Oasis, spawning bizarre deviate flora, unnatural beasts, and the rogue Druids of the Fang.",["q886.5","q886.6"]]],"zone:deadmines":[["zone:deadmines#section3","The Secret of Ironclad Cove","Beyond the sweltering Goblin Foundry and the timber-strewn Mast Room lies Ironclad Cove, a vast subterranean grotto hollowed out beneath the Dagger Hills. In this hidden harbor, VanCleef has employed goblin shipbuilders and tinkerers to construct a colossal ironclad dreadnaught. Commanded by Captain Greenskin and First Mate Mr. Smite, this warship is engineered to sail through an underwater sea gate leading out toward the ocean, where the Defias Brotherhood means to prey on the sea trade that keeps Stormwind rich.",["defias.juggernaut"]]],"zone:duskwood":[["zone:duskwood#section2","The Scythe and the Wolf-Men","The land's modern nightmare deepened with the arrival of a night elf priestess named Velinde Starsong, who carried an otherworldly artifact known as the Scythe of Elune. When she vanished within the disused mine called Roland's Doom, the relic was eventually unearthed by a prospecting laborer named Jitters. The careless handling of the weapon unleashed ravenous, bipedal wolf-beasts known as worgen into the woods. Clad in perpetual mist and bloodlust, these extradimensional predators tore through the western groves, compounding the miseries of a region already groaning under undead curses.",["q1022.6"]]],"zone:elwynn":[["zone:elwynn#section3","The Red Bandana Threat","The Defias Brotherhood has spread through Elwynn as an organized band of outlaws. Its members came from Edwin VanCleef’s Stonemasons Guild after bitter disputes over pay with the House of Nobles. They have taken the Northshire Vineyards, the Brackwell Pumpkin Patch, Jerod’s Landing, and Mirror Lake Orchard. Rumors say their agents are guiding nearby gnolls and kobolds to unsettle the realm. Guards Rolf and Malakai went to investigate murlocs near Stone Cairn Lake; both were killed and left on its banks.",["defias.masons"]]],"zone:hallofthanes":[["zone:hallofthanes#section3","The Threat of Dirgehammer","Within the heart of the complex lies a formidable assembly of threats, from enchanted guardians like Plunder and the fiery brute Magmatus to rogue figures of ancient dwarven lineage such as Faldrim Anvilmar. Presiding over the inner sanctuary is Durgen Dirgehammer, whose defeat finally quells the growing unrest simmering beneath the city floor and secures the sacred dwarven halls from subversion.",["q96392.4"]]],"zone:ragefire":[["zone:ragefire#section3","The Warchief's Game","Neeru Fireblade, an ostensible warlock trainer dwelling beside the chasm's entrance in the Cleft of Shadow, secretly serves as the local leader of the Burning Blade. Neeru sends Horde adventurers into the depths under the guise of loyalty tests, hoping to cull weak cultists and eliminate Thrall's supporters. However, Thrall is fully aware of Neeru's deception. The Warchief uses these forays to eliminate dangerous cult leaders like Jergosh and Bazzalan while letting Neeru remain in place to monitor the Shadow Council's broader schemes.",["q5726.2","q5726.3"]]],"zone:silverpine":[["zone:silverpine#section2","Rival Enclaves and the Scourge","The Forsaken claim to Silverpine is hotly contested by living and undead factions alike. To the east on Fenris Isle, Thule Ravenclaw leads a force of Scourge remnants while commanding the plague-addled Rot Hide gnolls that plague northern farmlands. In the south, human mages from Dalaran hold the fortified settlement of Ambermill, conducting mystical research and opposing Forsaken expansion. The Royal Apothecary Society and High Executor Hadrec at the Sepulcher wage a brutal, multifaceted campaign to eliminate these threats and test virulent new plagues.",["q437.6"]],["zone:silverpine#section3","The Worgen and Shadowfang","Silverpine's most infamous peril is the worgen curse. High above the southwest woods sits Shadowfang Keep, the former seat of Baron Silverlaine, now dominated by the mad Dalaran archmage Arugal. Seeking to repel the Scourge during the Third War, Arugal summoned extra-dimensional wolf-beasts. The creatures turned on their summoners, spreading a lycanthropic curse across the southern woods. In Pyrewood Village, ordinary-looking human villagers go about their chores by daylight, but transform into savage worgen beneath the night sky.",["q421.5"]]],"zone:stockade":[["zone:stockade#section2","Nobles and Assassins","The riot laid bare the ugly underbelly of Stormwind's political machine. Outside territories had long grown frustrated with the House of Nobles halting executions for their own leverage. Redridge guard Berton wanted the captured orc Targorr the Dread executed for atrocities, only to watch nobles freeze the sentence. Darkshire councilman Millstipe watched the grave-robber Dextren Ward get spared the gallows despite desecrating graves for the necromancer Morbent Fel. Even the dwarves of Dun Modr sent word demanding the death of Dark Iron saboteur Kam Deepfury. With the Stockade in chaos, local authorities quietly hired adventurers to carry out the executions the capital's courts refused to enforce.",["q303.3"]]],"zone:stormwind":[["zone:stormwind#section4","Where the Masons Went","The stonemasons gathered again in Westfall under their guildmaster, Edwin VanCleef. There they became the Defias Brotherhood, red-masked bandits now strangling the kingdom’s farmland. The builders of Stormwind’s white walls have sworn to make the House of Nobles pay for every stone.",["defias.masons"]],["zone:stormwind#section5","A Friend at Court","The Defias Brotherhood found help within Stormwind Keep. Lord Gregor Lescovar of the House of Nobles secretly dealt with the Brotherhood, passing along the kingdom’s plans while sitting among the lords who had cheated the stonemasons.",["unsent.lescovar"]],["zone:stormwind#section6","Shadows in the Keep","Lady Katrana Prestor has secretly guided the House of Nobles’ decisions. She inflamed its conflict with the stonemasons and ignored pleas for military aid from settlements such as Westfall and Redridge. She also whispered poison to Highlord Bolvar, keeping Stormwind’s legions idle and divided as unrest beyond the city walls threatens the throne.",["prestor.onyxia"]]],"zone:westfall":[["zone:westfall#section4","Masons in Red Masks","Edwin VanCleef leads the Brotherhood. He was the guildmaster who rebuilt Stormwind City, but the House of Nobles denied his stonemasons their pay, and riots drove them out. They took up the red mask in Westfall. The bandits burning its fields are the same hands that raised the capital's walls.",["defias.masons"]],["zone:westfall#section5","The Deadmines Conspiracy","Beneath Moonbrook, Edwin VanCleef has gathered cutthroats, goblins, and renegades in the abandoned gold shafts of the Deadmines. The Brotherhood is building a massive ironclad juggernaut there, meant to sail up the coast and bombard Stormwind City.",["defias.masons","defias.hideout","defias.juggernaut"]]]};
